{"term": "крутить роман", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "krutit' roman", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "romantic", "concept": "love_affair", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "euphemism", "crude": 5, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To spin or conduct a romance; a Russian idiom for having a love affair or romantic liaison, implying ongoing romantic and typically sexual activity.", "english_term": "to have a romance / to carry on an affair", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "The verb 'крутить' (to twist/spin/manage) suggests deliberate cultivation of the romance rather than passive passion; does not necessarily imply infidelity the way 'affair' often does in English — can describe a legitimate relationship."}
{"term": "любиться", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "lyubit'sya", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "intercourse_generic", "concept": "intercourse_romantic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 22, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To make love; the reflexive-reciprocal form of любить (to love), used in literary or elevated registers to mean engaging in sexual intercourse with mutual romantic feeling.", "english_term": "to make love", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "More explicitly sexual in many literary contexts than 'заниматься любовью'; retains a romantic literary register; common in 19th–20th century Russian prose; rare in casual modern speech; the reciprocal morphology encodes mutuality."}
{"term": "миловаться", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "milovat'sya", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "petting", "concept": "intimate_physical_play", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "literary", "crude": 8, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 78, "recognition_pct_peak": 78, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To caress and be tender with each other; a Russian literary term for affectionate physical intimacy including kissing, embracing, and gentle touching, typically stopping short of intercourse.", "english_term": "to cuddle and caress / to be affectionate", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'милый' (dear, sweet, darling); associated with tender romantic rather than explicitly sexual contexts; less common in contemporary speech than in 19th–20th century Russian literature; connotes sweetness rather than heat."}
{"term": "брать", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "brat'", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "intercourse_conquest", "concept": "intercourse_aggressive", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 48, "technical": 3, "recognition_pct_current": 92, "recognition_pct_peak": 92, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To take; used in Russian slang to mean to have sex with someone, with a strong connotation of masculine dominance or sexual conquest.", "english_term": "to take (sexually)", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Standard Russian verb 'to take' repurposed as sexual slang; directional and asymmetrical — the man 'takes' the woman, encoding a power asymmetry; semantic complement to 'дать'; not maximally vulgar but clearly sexual in context."}
{"term": "дать", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "dat'", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "sexual_availability", "concept": "intercourse_opportunistic", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 55, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 96, "recognition_pct_peak": 96, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To give; in Russian sexual slang, for a woman to consent to and engage in sex, framed as providing sexual access to a male partner.", "english_term": "to put out / to give it up", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Semantic complement to 'брать': the man 'takes,' the woman 'gives.' Used in phrases like 'она ему дала' (she gave it to him). Not maximally vulgar but clearly sexual in context; frames sex as a commodity provided by women and extracted by men; widely understood across all demographics."}
{"term": "потрогать", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "potrogat'", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "manual", "concept": "manual_stimulation", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 25, "technical": 5, "recognition_pct_current": 96, "recognition_pct_peak": 96, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To touch or feel up; in sexual contexts, to touch a partner's body or genitals with the hands for erotic purposes.", "english_term": "to feel up / to touch", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Literally just 'to touch once' (perfective); sexual meaning is context-dependent; can imply unauthorized groping as readily as consensual manual stimulation; lacks the specificity of genital-only contact."}
{"term": "обжиматься", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "obzhimat'sya", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "petting", "concept": "making_out", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 15, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 93, "recognition_pct_peak": 93, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To embrace and kiss passionately; a Russian colloquial term for making out, involving sustained kissing and close physical contact stopping short of intercourse.", "english_term": "to make out / to neck", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'обжать' (to press around / hug tightly); implies more physical intensity than a simple embrace; commonly used among teenagers and young adults; does not necessarily imply genital contact; often describes couples in public places."}
{"term": "тискаться", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "tiskat'sya", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "petting", "concept": "heavy_petting", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 28, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 88, "recognition_pct_peak": 88, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To fondle and squeeze each other; a Russian colloquial term for mutual groping and heavy petting, typically implying breast or genital contact stopping short of intercourse.", "english_term": "to grope each other / heavy petting", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'тискать' (to squeeze, to press firmly); implies more physical intensity and genital proximity than 'обжиматься'; often used to describe youthful sexual exploration or foreplay that does not progress to intercourse."}
{"term": "нежничать", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "nezhnuchat'", "category": "euphemisms_idioms", "subcategory": "petting", "concept": "intimate_physical_play", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "slang_neutral", "crude": 5, "technical": 2, "recognition_pct_current": 82, "recognition_pct_peak": 82, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "To be tender and gentle with each other; a Russian colloquial term for soft, affectionate physical intimacy including light touching, cuddling, and caressing.", "english_term": "to be tender / to cuddle", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "From 'нежный' (tender, gentle, soft); the softest entry in the physical intimacy spectrum — rarely implies intercourse; suggests sweet, playful affection and emotional warmth rather than explicitly sexual contact."}
{"term": "блуд", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "blud", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "moral_condemnation", "concept": "fornication", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 30, "technical": 20, "recognition_pct_current": 73, "recognition_pct_peak": 73, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Fornication, lechery, or sexual immorality broadly; an Old Church Slavonic-derived term denoting illicit sexual activity with heavy moral and religious condemnation.", "english_term": "fornication / debauchery / lechery", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Root of 'блудница' (harlot), 'блудить' (to fornicate / to err / to wander), and 'заблудиться' (to get lost — same root, showing how 'wandering' and 'sexual straying' were conceptually linked); found primarily in Orthodox religious texts, older literature, and ironic contemporary usage; broader in scope than English 'fornication' — can encompass all illicit sexuality."}
{"term": "прелюбодеяние", "lang": "ru", "script": "cyrl", "romanization": "prelyubodeyaniye", "category": "archaic_literary", "subcategory": "adultery", "concept": "fornication", "is_novel_concept": false, "register": "moral_religious", "crude": 15, "technical": 70, "recognition_pct_current": 80, "recognition_pct_peak": 80, "peak_year": null, "regional": null, "definition": "Adultery; the Church Slavonic and formal Russian legal-theological term for sexual intercourse by a married person with someone other than their spouse.", "english_term": "adultery", "english_translation_quality": "approximate", "notes": "Mapped to 'fornication' as the closest registry concept, though прелюбодеяние specifically denotes adultery (at least one party married), not sex outside marriage generally; appears in the Seventh Commandment ('Не прелюбодействуй'), in pre-revolutionary Russian law, and in Orthodox canon law; the 'пре-' prefix (beyond/excess) combined with 'любо' (love) encodes 'love transgressed beyond its proper bounds.'"}
